# docs/projects/ — per-project memory

## Abstract

One folder per company or project, holding everything we know about it: the
current state, the chronology, one note per meeting, the durable dossier, and
the documents we sent them. This is the operator's **written memory** — the
place a new session reads to stop being a goldfish.

The shape is not a matter of taste. `cs project new <name>` stamps it, so every
project in every `<company>-cs` clone looks the same and an agent can find its
way without being told. Read the conventions below before adding a file by hand.

## Creating a project

```bash
.venv/bin/python -m cs project new <name>          # name = folder slug
.venv/bin/python -m cs project new <name> --title "Full Company Name S.p.A."
```

`<name>` is a lowercase slug (`acme-corp`), not a person's first name and not an
email address. The command refuses to touch a folder that already exists, so it
is safe to run when you are unsure whether the project is already there.

## The shape of a project folder

```
docs/projects/<name>/
├── README.md      what this project is, and the index of its own files
├── status.md      where we are NOW: agreed scope, what we owe, deadlines, risks
├── timeline.md    the verified chronology, with a source per entry
├── meetings/      one file per meeting: YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>.md
└── <name>.md      the durable dossier (optional): the company, the people,
                   the research. Add it when there is enough to warrant it.
```

Plus whatever the project itself accumulates: proposals, analyses, PDFs, DOCX,
saved attachments.

| File | Owns | Volatility |
|------|------|------------|
| `README.md` | What the project is; the index of the folder | Low |
| `status.md` | The present. **Wins on "what is the current state"** | High — update on every contact |
| `timeline.md` | What happened, when, and how we know. The chronological index into `meetings/` | Append-only |
| `meetings/*` | One meeting each: who was there, what was decided, what was asked of us | Append-only, never rewritten |
| `<name>.md` | Durable facts: the company's numbers, the people, the research. **Wins on durable company facts** | Low |

Two rules keep this from rotting:

**`status.md` is the only place that describes the present.** If the dossier
also carries a "where we are" section, they will disagree within a month and a
reader cannot tell which is stale. State goes in one file.

**Meetings are append-only.** A meeting note records what happened at a point in
time. If we later learn we misread the room, add a dated addendum at the bottom —
never edit the body. Otherwise the memory quietly rewrites its own history and we
lose the ability to see that we were wrong.

## How to write

**Every file opens with front matter, then `## Abstract`.** The abstract is a
short paragraph saying what the document is and what it currently claims, so a
reader — human or agent — can decide in ten seconds whether to read on. Files
`cs project new` stamps already have both; keep them when you edit.

**Mark how much you trust each fact.** A dossier that does not distinguish what
we verified from what we assumed will eventually be quoted back to a customer.

| Marker | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `[confirmed]` | Verified against primary sources, or independent sources that concur |
| `[mail]` | From a real mail in the engine archive — cite date and sender |
| `[meeting]` | From a meeting, i.e. from somebody's debrief |
| `[inferred]` | One weak source, or our own inference |
| `[reported]` | An authoritative source says so, but we could not verify it |
| `[to verify]` | We are acting on it, but it is not established |

**Language is English**, these notes included. The exceptions are copy written
for the customer, which follows the customer's own language, and **verbatim
quotes**, which stay in the language they were said in — quote first, gloss in
English if the meaning is not obvious.

**A new meeting** starts from [`_meeting-template.md`](_meeting-template.md):

```bash
cp docs/projects/_meeting-template.md docs/projects/<name>/meetings/2026-01-31-kickoff.md
```

Then add a line for it in that project's `timeline.md`, so it can be found from
the chronology.

**No secrets.** These files are tracked in git (private repo
`hahnbanach/{{ company_slug }}-cs`) for multi-machine sync. Credentials, tokens
and passwords never go in here.

**Customer codebases stay outside.** A repo owned by the customer (e.g.
`~/acme-corp/`) lives where it lives; the dossier only records that it exists and
where.

## The two layers — files and engine memory

The `mrcall-desktop` engine holds the **live** layer: synced mail, entities,
tasks. These files hold the **durable** layer: what was decided, what we
concluded, what we owe. You need both, and they answer different questions.

- **Mail is the engine's, and the engine's is ground truth.** Never transcribe a
  mail from memory. Read it:
  `cs --account <acct> rpc emails.search '{"query":"…","folder":"all","limit":60}'`
- **Judgment is the files'.** A decision taken in a meeting, a piece of research,
  a read on a person — that never arrives by mail, so it exists only if somebody
  writes it here.
- **Where they disagree about history, the files win** — they are versioned and
  reviewable. Where they disagree about mail, the engine wins.
- Facts worth having in both places can be pushed into engine memory with
  `cs chat --allow create_memory "<atomic fact, with date and source>"`. Treat it
  as fire-and-forget and **verify with `cs ask`**: the turn persists engine-side
  but does not always return cleanly to the client.

## Agent procedure — when asked about a project or customer

**Mandatory before answering**, even when a folder already exists here. Reading
only `docs/projects/` is the goldfish trap; reading only the engine loses every
conclusion we ever drew.

1. **The project's own memory:** `docs/projects/<name>/status.md` first, then
   `timeline.md`, then the meeting notes that matter and the dossier.
2. **Engine memory**, on the account that owns the relationship:
   ```bash
   .venv/bin/python -m cs --account {{ founder_sweep_account if founder_sweep_enabled else email_address }} ask "<company / contacts / status / deadlines>"
   .venv/bin/python -m cs ask "<same question>"   # the {{ accounts_default }} default, if it might be there
   ```
   Many business relationships live on **{{ founder_sweep_account if founder_sweep_enabled else email_address }}** rather than on
   `{{ accounts_default }}` (the autonomous loop's default). Names in
   `cs accounts` are mail addresses, never a bare first name.
3. **Key contacts:** `cs --account <acct> dossier <email>` — threads, tasks, CRM.
4. **Answer from both layers**, and say which is stale when they disagree. Then
   fix the stale one before closing the work.

**Run `cs` from the clone root.** The manifest and env chain resolve from the
working directory: from anywhere else `--account` fails with *"unknown --account
… Configured: (none)"*.

**`--account` switches the ENGINE profile only.** `cs contacted` and
`cs unanswered` read Gmail over IMAP with the operator's single credential
({{ email_address }}), so on any other account they answer for
{{ email_address }} — a false "never contacted", exit code 1. `cs thread` and
`cs ask` do honour `--account`, because they go through the engine. Use those to
check whether a thread has been answered on a non-default mailbox.

Twin rule in `CLAUDE.md` §9, loaded on every agent session.
